# The one walker every stage runs through: a rewrite applied to a node, and the metadata carried over (notes/wap.md
# "The pipeline").
#
# A stage only says what a node becomes. Where the result was written is never its business: `staged` gives the
# kernel form a rewrite built the node it was built from as its origin, so an error found three stages later still
# points at the user's line and quotes what the user wrote:
#
#   print 3                     the phrase, written at line 2
#   → Call(print, [Literal(3)]) the Call's origin is the phrase; the Literal's, staged on its own, is the 3
#
# A form that has an origin already was made by an inner stage, closer to the code: it keeps it. Later the walker also
# records the rule or stage that built the form, for errors like `line 4: unless … (rule extensions/…:3): …`.

# the form the rewrite builds of n, its origin n
staged(n: Node, rewrite: function) := {
	form = rewrite(n)
	if form.origin == ø { form.origin = n }
	form
}
